>[]($block.attrs('warning'))
>sqlz reads and replays migrations offline today, but it can't yet apply them
>to a database. Migration state, locking, `migrate upgrade` and
>`migrate downgrade` are planned for 0.1 and not implemented.

# [Revisions]($heading.id('revisions'))

Migrations form an Alembic-style graph. Each revision is a directory under
the configured `migrations` root with a manifest and its SQL:

```
migrations/
  aaaaaaaaaaaa_example_schema/
    revision.ziggy
    common.up.sql
    common.down.sql
```

```ziggy
.format_version = 1,
.revision = "aaaaaaaaaaaa",
.parents = [],
.description = "example schema",
.created_utc = "2026-09-16T12:00:00Z",
.backends = [.sqlite, .postgres],
.reversible = true,
.transaction = .{ .sqlite = .always, .postgres = .always },
```

`common.*.sql` applies to every listed backend. Revisions can carry
backend-specific SQL when the dialects differ.

# [Branches and merges]($heading.id('branches'))

A revision with several `.parents` merges branches. A revision without a
child is a head. The checker validates the graph (missing parents, cycles,
duplicate IDs) and orders it deterministically.

# [Offline replay]($heading.id('offline-replay'))

On every build the checker replays the supported DDL of the whole graph into
an in-memory catalog per backend. That catalog is the schema your queries are
checked against, so CI needs no database and no credentials.

The supported DDL subset and the planned runner are specified in the
[migrations design document](https://github.com/westack-it/sqlz/blob/main/docs/migrations.md).
